scope.reasoning:
The main points hold against full-text reading of both papers, the author bib files and the shipped Acme style:
- the ICSA 2022 venue and DOI;
- ROSDiscover checks only structural rules, with no ordering, startup or timing rule in the paper or in ROSFam.acme;
- ROSInfer infers state machines driven by subscriber callbacks, periodic triggers and component-started events;
- ROSInfer generates PlusCal/TLA+ with LTL checking;
- its formalism excludes message content;
- both papers name F Prime.

So the claim's use as a bound on the cFS note's novelty stands, and I do not refute it. It has these concrete inaccuracies or overstatements:

(1) The state-variable definition is wrong and too narrow. The paper's criterion is use in control conditions plus global or component scope. The code at 78301b86 requires assignment in any function other than the publisher. Neither requires assignment in a subscriber callback. This understates ROSInfer, so a cFS paper that differentiates against the narrower version would overclaim.

(2) 'Found 3 bugs' leaves out that these were three known, pre-selected dataset bugs re-detected, given a component list, an expected output and parameter values.

(3) The race-condition statement drops the authors' hedge 'most kinds of'. It is also a future-work remark about static analysis in general.

(4) ROSDiscover's paper names topic, service and action ports. 'Parameter ports' is an Acme-model detail, and action ports are omitted.

(5) The parenthetical rule list is the paper's four topic rules. The shipped Acme style has further structural rules (service self-call, parameter reader/writer, duplicate nodes, one bus per topic), still with nothing temporal.

(6) Both tools are scoped to ROS 1 C++. For ROSInfer this is an inference from its ROS 1 API patterns and its being an extension of ROSDiscover; the ROSInfer text does not say 'ROS 1'.

'Model-checks' is a reasonable reading but the paper only says 'checked'.

What I could not open: IEEE Xplore, Crossref and dblp were blocked, so the venue rests on author bib files plus the ROSInfer reference list.

recheck.reasoning:
I re-opened the cited PDF, which is byte-identical to the local copy, and read the whole extracted text against every cited line range. Most of the claim holds:
- the error can occur even when every shared access is lock-protected;
- the checker is an annotation-free ESC/Java extension using per-local ghost booleans stale_t and from_critical_t;
- it mostly ignores which lock is acquired;
- the table gives 617 kloc, 43 false alarms, 1 benign race and 4 bugs, and the column sums check out;
- staleness is tied to critical-section boundaries.

Two problems are imprecise rather than refuting, so refuted=false with a corrected claim.

(1) The paraphrased definition copies the paper's loose introduction sentence ("lock held when read, not held when later used"). The technique the paper actually defines does not flag a use after the lock is released. The paper explicitly calls such snapshot use justifiable. A value is flagged only after the thread enters a later critical section, including wait() re-entry, where the stale use happens while the lock is held again. This matters for the note's F3 framing: the precedent is about a snapshot taken in one critical section and used across a later synchronization boundary, not simply a value used outside the lock.

(2) The bibliographic data is partly unverified. MSR confirms CCPE vol. 16, October 2004, pp. 1161-1172, and an issue DOI ending v16:12. MSR's own BibTeX says number 2, which is inconsistent. The article DOI 10.1002/cpe.866 could not be resolved because every DOI and publisher endpoint was blocked.

The final sentence ("not physical age or producer publication") is a fair inference from the ghost-state rules and the critical-section-count age_t, but it should be labelled as inference.
